#### What it does
Wiping a Mac keyboard sets off random shortcuts and sends half typed messages. Keypause switches every key off for a time you choose, from 30 seconds to 10 minutes, then switches them back on by itself. The mouse and trackpad keep working, so unlocking is always one click away.

It is free and open source. You can try the lock in your browser on [the website](https://destucr.github.io/Keypause/) before you install it.

![Keypause locked: Shh, your keys are napping, with a timer under the notch](/images/keypause/keypause-locked.webp)

![Keypause when the timer ends: Good morning, your keys are back](/images/keypause/keypause-keys-back.webp)

#### How I built it
- **Every key off**: A CGEventTap swallows all keyboard input, including shortcuts, function keys and media keys, for as long as the timer runs.
- **Quick to start**: A system wide hotkey (Control, Option, Command and K) from anywhere, the menu bar icon, a keypause command in Terminal, and keypause:// links for Raycast and Shortcuts.
- **Calm while you wait**: A small panel under the notch shows the timer. The sky turns from night to sunrise as time runs out, and Kip, a sleepy keycap, wakes up when the keys come back.
- **Private**: It never reads, stores or sends what you type. The only network request is an optional update check against the public GitHub releases.
- **Shipped like a real app**: DMG and ZIP releases on GitHub, a Homebrew cask, an update check and launch at login.

#### Challenges
The lock has to be easy to leave. A keyboard that stays off is the worst way for a cleaning tool to fail, so the timer always ends the lock, and unlocking works with the mouse even if something goes wrong. The other hard part was making a utility feel friendly without getting in the way of the job.
